{"claims":[{"claim_text":"The Democratic Socialists of America's structure, culture, and organizing program must allow the organization to move through conflict to build a more democratic, comradely, and effective organization.","claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"The working class needs a mass party that can contest for state power.","claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[2]},{"claim_text":"The Democratic Socialists of America believes state power is a necessary and positive force for implementing democratic socialism.","claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[3]},{"claim_text":"The Democratic Socialists of America needs a working-class party—a democratic political organization that can organize a mass base of socialist voters, elect socialists to legislatures across the country, build caucuses of Socialists in Office, fight for transformational, working-class reforms, support further working-class organization, and eventually win the durable support of a working-class majority to implement a democratic socialist program.","claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[3]},{"claim_text":"The only viable strategy to build a party in the United States, at present, is by using the Democratic Party ballot line.","claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[4]},{"claim_text":"Starting a third party with its own ballot line from scratch has not been a viable strategy in the United States for a century, and is not a viable strategy in the current moment.","claim_types":["factual_assertion"],"evidence_ids":[5]},{"claim_text":"Giving up the Democratic ballot line to pursue third-party politics would be a strategic disaster for the Democratic Socialists of America and a historic error for working-class politics in the United States, given the organization's size and the United States' legally restrictive two-party system.","claim_types":["evaluation"],"evidence_ids":[5]}],"unresolved_references":[]}
```

